  "other_facts": "Newborns are at increased risk of jaundice, a condition in which excess bilirubin accumulates in blood. Left untreated, jaundice can lead to neurological impairment and death. Jaundice resulting from unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia is easily treated with exposure to blue light, and phototherapy systems have been developed for low-resource settings; however, there are no appropriate solutions to diagnose and monitor jaundice in these settings. BiliSpec is a low-cost reader and disposable lateral flow card designed to measure the concentration of total bilirubin from several drops of blood at the point of care. The clinical study was conducted at Queen Elizabeth Central Hospital in Blantyre, Malawi. Bilirubin concentrations in patient samples ranged from 1.1 mg/dL to 23.0 mg/dL. Blood from normal volunteers was spiked with varying amounts of bilirubin for the in vitro evaluation.",
  "summary": "A pilot clinical study evaluated BiliSpec, a low-cost point-of-care reader for measuring total bilirubin in neonates at risk for jaundice, showing strong correlation with laboratory reference standards."
}
