  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Glucose tolerance categories were defined at baseline and at a second visit 3 years later: normal fasting glucose (NFG), normal glucose tolerance (NGT), NFG/NGT, impaired fasting glucose (IFG), impaired glucose tolerance (IGT), and IFG/IGT. Change categories were regression to normoglycemia, remaining in previous status, and progression to diabetes. Cox proportional hazards models were adjusted for traditional risk factors and their changes. During follow-up, 428 participants experienced CVD, including 265 men. For the shift from NFG/NGT to IFG/IGT compared with persistent NFG/NGT, P=0.048. For the shift from IFG/IGT to diabetes compared with persistent IFG/IGT, P=0.04 for CVD and P=0.02 for CHD. For regression from IFG/IGT to normoglycemia compared with persistent IFG/IGT, P=0.52 for CVD and P=0.85 for CHD. In an insulin-data subgroup of 803 participants, insulin resistance converting to diabetes had P=0.01 for CVD and P=0.05 for CHD. Shifts from IFG, IGT, and IFG/IGT to diabetes showed increased CVD/CHD risk, but statistical significance was reported only for IFG/IGT. Persistent IFG/IGT was not associated with higher risk compared with reversion to normoglycemia, and conversion from NFG/NGT to IFG/IGT showed a signal for lower CVD/CHD risk.",
  "summary": "In a cohort of 4094 Iranian adults aged >=30 years without diabetes or cardiovascular disease at enrolment, progression from IFG/IGT to diabetes increased incident CVD/CHD risk, persistent IFG/IGT did not confer higher risk than regression to normoglycemia, and transition from NFG/NGT to IFG/IGT showed a lower CVD risk signal."
}

