  "other_facts": "No method is currently available to identify agalA mutations determining clinically relevant Fabry disease; the study was described as the largest European Fabry cohort and investigated whether a defect-specific biomarker could stratify persons at risk. Participants underwent comprehensive clinical workup, genetic analysis, and laboratory testing including agalA activity and lyso-Gb3, a degradation product of accumulating Gb3. Extensive family screening with clinical workup of relatives was performed. Classical mutations were known to cause classic Fabry disease with specific symptoms and high risk for major events in heart, kidney, and central nervous system; atypical mutations lacked typical presentation. All 17 patients with atypical mutations had lower lyso-Gb3 levels than any of the 55 patients with classical Fabry disease. Clinical investigation blinded to lyso-Gb3 results found no classic organ involvement in the 6 novel-mutation patients with lyso-Gb3 <2.7 ng/mL or their relatives. The biomarker lyso-Gb3 may identify clinically relevant agalA mutations leading to Fabry disease.",
  "summary": "In 124 individuals with agalA mutations, lyso-Gb3 using a 2.7 ng/mL cutoff distinguished clinically relevant classical Fabry disease mutations from atypical or novel mutations."
}

