  "other_facts": "Cardiorespiratory monitoring is used in the NICU to assess the clinical status of newborn infants and detect critical deteriorations in cardiorespiratory function. Currently, heart rate is monitored by electrocardiography and respiration by chest impedance. Disadvantages of current monitoring techniques are usage of wired adhesive electrodes which may damage the skin and hinder care. The Bambi® belt is a wireless and non-adhesive alternative that enables cardiorespiratory monitoring by measuring electrical activity of the diaphragm via transcutaneous electromyography. A previous study showed feasibility of the Bambi® belt. The study will be performed in the NICU of the Máxima Medical Center in Veldhoven and the Emma Children's Hospital, Amsterdam University Medical Centre in Amsterdam, The Netherlands. The protocol was approved by the Medical Ethical Committee of the MMC and the Central Committee for Human Research. MMC started patient recruitment in July 2021 and AmsterdamUMC in August 2021. The results will be presented at conferences and published in peer-reviewed journals. Trial registration number: NL9480.",
  "summary": "This multicentre non-inferiority paired study protocol will evaluate the Bambi® belt compared with standard electrocardiography and chest impedance for cardiorespiratory monitoring in 39 NICU infants monitored for 24 hours."
}

