  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Approximately 44% of community residents participated (77 of 175 adults). Samples of hair, blood, and/or urine were collected. Samples were analyzed for contaminants (heavy metals and persistent organic pollutants) and nutrients (trace elements and omega-3 fatty acids). Levels were compared to health-based guidance values and results from other Canadian biomonitoring projects. More than 95% of participants had levels of lead, cadmium, and mercury below select health-based guidance values. Elevated levels of some contaminants (lead, cobalt, manganese, hexachlorobenzene) were observed compared to the general Canadian population. Levels of other POPs (DDT, DDE, PCBs) were similar to or lower than those in the general Canadian population. The study aims to evaluate effectiveness of international initiatives like the Stockholm Convention and Minamata Convention. It complements regional environmental monitoring and informs local and regional traditional food consumption advisories.",
  "summary": "This human biomonitoring project in Old Crow, Yukon, measured contaminant and nutrient biomarkers in 77 adults in 2019, finding levels of lead, cadmium, and mercury below health-based guidance values for most participants, but elevated levels of some contaminants compared to the general Canadian population."
}

