  "other_facts": "The incidence of severe CTCAE grade 3 or 4 immune-related hepatitis under combination ipilimumab and nivolumab ranges from 15% to 20%; current guidelines recommend systemic corticosteroids followed by second-line immunosuppressants such as mycophenolate mofetil; infliximab is commonly administered for other immune-related adverse events such as colitis but is generally not recommended for immune-related hepatitis; a recent single-center study reported infliximab use in 10 patients with steroid-refractory immune-related hepatitis without signs of infliximab-associated hepatotoxicity and overall favorable efficacy; the study aimed to evaluate the safety and efficacy of infliximab in steroid-refractory immune-related hepatitis; patients were included from five university hospitals in Germany and one in Belgium; CTCAE version 5.0 was used; infliximab was administered intravenously; median interval between onset of immune-related hepatitis and first infliximab dose was 23 days.",
  "summary": "In a multicenter retrospective analysis of 28 patients with advanced cutaneous melanoma and steroid-refractory immune-related hepatitis after ipilimumab plus nivolumab, intravenous infliximab 5 mg/kg was associated with high remission rates, corticosteroid tapering, and no infliximab-attributable hepatotoxicity or deaths."
