  "other_facts": "Background: the combination of PESI and troponin testing could help physicians identify appropriate patients with acute pulmonary embolism for early hospital discharge. Single-center registry. PESI classified patients into five classes (I-V). cTnI threshold was 0.1 ng/mL. Four groups were defined as: group 1 PESI I-II plus cTnI < 0.1 ng/mL; group 2 PESI III-V plus cTnI < 0.1 ng/mL; group 3 PESI I-II plus cTnI >= 0.1 ng/mL; group 4 PESI III-V plus cTnI >= 0.1 ng/mL. The addition of non-elevated cTnI to low-risk PESI did not improve the negative predictive value or negative likelihood ratio compared with either test alone. PESI classification more accurately identified patients with PE who are at low risk of all-cause death within 30 days of presentation compared with cTnI testing.",
  "summary": "In a prospective cohort of 567 patients with objectively confirmed acute symptomatic pulmonary embolism, PESI classification more accurately identified patients at low risk of 30-day all-cause mortality than troponin I testing, with overall 30-day mortality of 10%."
}

