    "duration": "24 weeks (6 months)"
  },
  "outcomes": [
    {
      "measure": "End of Treatment Complete Response (EOTCR)",
      "timepoint": "End of treatment (24 weeks)",
      "effect": "81.6% (155 out of 190)",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "Nonresponders (NR)",
      "timepoint": "End of treatment (24 weeks)",
      "effect": "18.4% (35 out of 190)",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "Sustained Virological Response (SVR)",
      "timepoint": "6 months after completion of treatment",
      "effect": "72.25% (112 out of 155)",
      "ci": null
    },
    {
      "measure": "Relapsers",
      "timepoint": "6 months after completion of treatment",
      "effect": "27.7% (43 out of 155)",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "HCV infects 130 to 170 million (2.2-3%) of the world's population and is a main cause of chronic liver disease, fibrosis, cirrhosis, liver cancer, and liver transplantation. The study was conducted at the Department of Medicine, KRL General Hospital Islamabad. Non-probability purposive sampling was used. Written and informed consent was obtained. A specially designed performa containing the patient profile, family transmission, and baseline laboratory values was filled. Chi-Square tests were used for analysis. 60 patients were excluded (30 did not meet inclusion criteria, 23 lost to follow-up, 7 declined treatment). Response rates were correlated with gender, baseline ALT, necro-inflammatory stage assessed by liver biopsy, probable risk factors, and family history. The SVR was lower compared to previous international and local data.",
  "summary": "A quasi-experimental study of 190 treatment-naïve Hepatitis C genotype 3a patients found that 24 weeks of standard Interferon alpha 2a and Ribavirin therapy yielded an 81.6% end-of-treatment complete response rate and a 72.25% sustained virological response rate, which was lower than previously reported data."
}

