  "other_facts": "Objective: evaluate long-term efficacy and safety of once-daily enoxaparin plus warfarin for outpatient ambulatory treatment of lower-limb DVT. The trial was conducted at 14 centers in Turkey. Enoxaparin was administered subcutaneously until INR reached 2-3. Efficacy parameters included clinical signs (leg circumference), symptoms (edema, pain, tenderness), recanalization rates upon duplex ultrasound examination, laboratory findings (D-dimer and INR levels), and postthrombotic syndrome status with CEAP classification, evaluated every 3 months during 18 months of follow-up. Safety endpoints included minor bleeding, major bleeding, and serious adverse events. The authors concluded ambulatory treatment was effective in symptomatic healing and clinical improvement by reducing thrombus formation and organization at all levels of the lower extremity venous system, without significant major bleeding risk, and consistent with American College of Chest Physicians 1A level evidence.",
  "summary": "In an open-label multicenter phase IV trial of 246 outpatients with symptomatic lower-extremity DVT, once-daily enoxaparin followed by warfarin for at least 3 months with compression stockings improved symptoms, leg circumference, and iliofemoral recanalization over 18 months with few major bleeding events."
