  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Mammary tumor development is described as an age-associated phenomenon likely due to deficits in neuroendocrine-immune interactions. L-deprenyl is a monoamine oxidase-B inhibitor that previously enhanced immune responses and restored splenic noradrenergic innervation in rats with carcinogen-induced and spontaneously developing mammary tumors. The study objectives were to determine whether treating early middle-aged female rats prevents spontaneous mammary tumors with restoration of splenic and draining lymph node immunity and splenic sympathetic noradrenergic innervation, and whether deprenyl influences proliferation of ER-positive and ER-negative human breast cancer cells. Rats received 0 mg/kg (saline control), 1.0 mg/kg, or 2.5 mg/kg deprenyl daily intraperitoneally for 12 months. Cell experiments used media or 10^-3 to 10^-8 M deprenyl for 1, 2, 4, or 6 days. The authors concluded that mammary tumor development may be mediated through loss of immunity and sympathetic noradrenergic nerve fibers with reduced splenic norepinephrine, that deprenyl prevention may involve reversal of tumor-associated declines in sympathetic noradrenergic activity and cell-mediated immune responses, and that antitumor effects may be partially mediated through estrogen receptor-dependent intracellular signaling pathways.",
  "summary": "In early middle-aged female Sprague-Dawley rats, 12 months of deprenyl reduced spontaneous mammary tumor incidence and restored splenic noradrenergic and immune markers, while deprenyl partly inhibited ER-positive but not ER-negative human breast cancer cell proliferation in vitro."
