  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Objective: evaluate the efficacy of intraoperative quadratus lumborum block and compare it with other regional anesthetic techniques used in colorectal surgery patients at the same institution; setting: tertiary care academic medical center; study period: September 2022 to September 2024; procedures: minimally invasive laparoscopic or robotic colectomy or low anterior resection; surgical approach distribution: 82.1% laparoscopic; opioid status: 93.7% opioid-naive; treatment groups: no regional block 43/224 (19.2%), transversus abdominis plane block alone 69/224 (30.8%), transversus abdominis plane + quadratus lumborum block 112/224 (50.0%); intervention context: surgeon-administered quadratus lumborum block added to traditional transversus abdominis plane block within an enhanced recovery after surgery program; outcomes evaluated but not fully reported numerically: subjective pain scores in postanesthesia care unit and on postoperative days 1 and 2, need for intravenous hydromorphone, early mobilization, and return of bowel function; background: multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia is established in enhanced recovery after surgery protocols for colorectal surgery, commonly used regional techniques include transversus abdominis plane blocks, rectus sheath blocks, and continuous wound infiltration/catheters, quadratus lumborum block has been suggested to provide better visceral analgesia, resembles a posterior transversus abdominis plane block, and targets ventral rami of spinal nerve roots including subcostal and ilio-hypogastric nerves; limitations: retrospective single-institution design subject to selection bias, perioperative management not fully standardized including variability in ketorolac administration not captured in adjusted analyses, and discharge practices varied across providers potentially affecting length of stay; additional conclusion: randomized studies should be conducted to confirm benefits of transversus abdominis plane + quadratus lumborum block in minimally invasive colorectal surgery; video abstract available.",
  "summary": "In this retrospective observational study of 224 adults undergoing minimally invasive colorectal surgery, adding surgeon-administered quadratus lumborum block to transversus abdominis plane block was associated with lower postoperative day 2 opioid use and pain scores, shorter length of stay, and earlier return of bowel function."
}

