  "other_facts": "Hysteroscopy is a minimally invasive gynecologic technique widely practiced in outpatient procedures, and anesthesia choice is a key factor for surgical outcome and postoperative recovery. The study assessed different anesthetic modalities based on dexmedetomidine in outpatient hysteroscopic surgery anesthesia. Pubmed, Embase, and Cochrane-Library were searched from database inception to December 31, 2021. Duplicate literature was excluded and screened separately at three tiers: article title, abstract, and full text. Categorical variables were expressed as ORR Ratio (95% CI) and continuous variables were expressed as Mean Difference (95% CI). Data collation and analyses were performed using the gemtc package in R. Four trials were included, with three anesthetic drugs and five anesthetic modalities. A fixed-effects model was used for adverse events, respiratory depression, operative time, and post-anesthesia care unit time without significant heterogeneity (all I2<20%). Remimazolam tosylate showed safety benefits with a similar duration of post-anesthesia care unit stay versus dexmedetomidine. The authors concluded that anesthetic drug choice in outpatient surgery requires consideration of patient conditions and preferences.",
  "summary": "This systematic review and network meta-analysis of four trials and 301 participants found that dexmedetomidine was associated with more adverse events and respiratory depression but shorter operative and post-anesthesia care unit times compared with remimazolam tosylate and propofol in outpatient hysteroscopic surgery anesthesia."
