  "other_facts": "Despite revascularisation, a large proportion of ACS patients continue to experience MACEs, which are worsened by diabetes mellitus. Fibrinogen is a risk factor for MACEs in coronary artery disease and is often elevated in diabetes mellitus. The relationships between FIB, glucose metabolism (HbA1c and FBG), and MACEs following PCI in DM, non-DM, or whole ACS patients remained unknown. Participants were divided into three groups according to FIB level: FIB-L, FIB-M, and FIB-H, and baseline characteristics and MACEs were compared. Linear regression analysis of the relationship between glucose metabolism and FIB, plus Cox regression, survival analysis, and landmark analysis of MACEs, were performed. Elevated baseline FIB levels may be an important and independent predictor of MACEs following PCI, especially amongst those with DM. As the follow-up period increased, baseline FIB levels lost their ability to predict MACEs. The study was from China.",
  "summary": "In 411 ACS patients undergoing PCI, elevated baseline fibrinogen was positively associated with glucose metabolism and predicted MACEs, especially in patients with diabetes, but its predictive value diminished after the first 30 months."
