  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "TPMT phenotype analysis is established as a routine pharmacogenomic test before initiating thiopurine drug therapy. Conventionally measured TPMT activity is corrected for red blood cell parameters. Hb concentrations were compared in consecutive TPMT phenotype samples stratified into high (n = 111) and normal (n = 50) Hb-corrected enzyme activity. TPMT activity was also measured in samples received for full blood count determination, stratified into low (n = 50) and normal (n = 50) Hb. A reference interval for TPMT activity in mU/L was derived from a correlation between activity expressed in conventional units and mU/L (n = 1563), supported by comparison with associated genotype (n = 201). Samples with low Hb concentration had significantly higher Hb-corrected TPMT activity than samples with normal Hb concentration (P < 0.0001), suggesting misleading high Hb-corrected TPMT activity in anaemic patients. Expressing TPMT activity in mU/L can overcome misleading high Hb-corrected TPMT results occurring in patients with anaemia, which could lead to inappropriate treatment. Removing the need to measure RBC indices further simplifies TPMT phenotyping, leading to a more robust assay, with reduced turn-around time and cost.",
  "summary": "Expressing TPMT activity in mU/L whole blood without haemoglobin correction provides comparable phenotype-genotype concordance and phenotype distribution to the conventional Hb-corrected assay while avoiding misleadingly high TPMT activity results in anaemic patients."
