  "other_facts": "Cardiovascular drift is a progressive increase in heart rate and decrease in stroke volume during prolonged exercise, exacerbated by heat stress and thermal strain, and often accompanied by a decrease in work capacity indexed as maximal oxygen uptake. NIOSH recommends work:rest ratios to attenuate physiological strain during work in the heat. The study tested whether a recommended 45:15 min work:rest ratio during moderate work in hot conditions would cause cardiovascular drift to accumulate over consecutive work:rest cycles and cause decrements in VO2max. Environmental condition: indoor wet-bulb globe temperature = 29.0 ± 0.6 °C. Work intensity: 201-300 kcal/h. Protocol: two 45:15 min work:rest cycles; cardiovascular drift was evaluated at 15 and 45 min of each work bout; VO2max was measured after 120 min and, on a separate day, after 15 min under identical conditions for comparison before and after cardiovascular drift occurred. Conclusion: recommended work:rest ratios preserved work capacity but did not prevent accumulation of cardiovascular and thermal strain.",
  "summary": "In eight adults performing 120 minutes of simulated moderate work in hot conditions using a 45:15 work:rest ratio, cardiovascular and thermal strain accumulated but VO2max and work capacity were preserved."
