  "other_facts": "Objective: To identify the clinical features and prognostic implications associated with the 6MWT in patients with RA. Statistical methods: Regression models (linear and logistic) were built to identify independent predictors of shorter 6MWT distance. Cox proportional models were used to study the association of 6MWT distance with cardiovascular outcomes. 6MWT tertiles: 126 patients walked > 405 m, 129 walked 345-405 m, and 132 walked < 345 m. Independent predictors of walking shorter distances: older age (> 55 years), elevated waist circumference, NT-pro BNP > 125 pg/mL, anemia, C-reactive protein >= 3 mg/dL, and troponin T >= 14 pg/mL. p-value for cardiovascular outcome: 0.006. Clinical associations with walking shorter distances: older age, abdominal obesity, anemia, cardiac dysfunction, and inflammation. The 6MWT is simple, reproducible, and inexpensive, easily performed in routine practice, and provides important information regarding the patients´ status and outcomes, enabling the monitorization of the therapeutic optimization of the various domains of the RA.",
  "summary": "In a cohort of 387 rheumatoid arthritis patients, walking less than 345 m in the 6-minute walk test was associated with a higher risk of cardiovascular hospitalization or death, with older age, abdominal obesity, anemia, cardiac dysfunction, and inflammation being independent predictors of shorter walking distances."
