  "safety": [],
  "other_facts": "Cystic Fibrosis (CF) is a potentially lethal genetic disorder. The most frequent mutation worldwide in the Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator (CFTR) gene is designated as the Delta F508 mutation. This mutation was found in only 33% of Pakistani patients studied previously. Common Pakistani mutations remain to be identified, requiring appropriate screening tools. Sweat chloride determinations remain the gold standard for diagnosing CF. The study was conducted at the Aga Khan University Hospital. Sweat chloride analysis positive value was defined as greater than 60 meq/L. Of the 3 patients with abnormal sweat chloride and deltaF508 mutations, 2/6 (33%) were homozygotes and 1 was a compound heterozygote. 79% of patients did not have a sweat test, making it difficult to assess whether this subset had CF with a different CFTR mutation or no CF. Sweat chloride analysis is critical to distinguish CF from other causes of severe pulmonary and pancreatic insufficiencies and to define patients requiring further analysis.",
  "summary": "This screening study of 57 pediatric patients suspected of having Cystic Fibrosis found a low positivity rate (10.6%) for the Delta F508 mutation, highlighting the critical importance of sweat chloride analysis for accurate diagnosis."
}

