  "other_facts": "Epicardial adipose tissue (EAT) is described as a new independent marker of coronary artery disease (CAD). The study aimed to investigate the relationship between epicardial fat thickness (EFT) and endothelial dysfunction (ED) in patients with type I diabetes mellitus (TIDM). Fasting plasma glucose (FPG), lipid panel, glycosylated hemoglobin (HbA1C), high-sensitive C-reactive protein (hsCRP), and fibrinogen levels were determined. EFT was measured via two-dimensional M-mode echocardiography. Endothelial function was assessed as flow-mediated dilatation (FMD) at the brachial artery using high-resolution ultrasound. P values: EFT difference P < 0.001; FMD difference P < 0.001; EFT-FMD correlation P < 0.001; EFT-hsCRP correlation P < 0.001; EFT-fibrinogen correlation P = 0.007.",
