  "other_facts": "Impacted fetal head is a challenging complication of cesarean section associated with significant morbidity; training opportunities for impacted fetal head have been reported as inconsistent and inadequate. Obstetricians and midwives collaborated with model-making company Limbs & Things (UK) to modify the original PROMPT Flex simulator and develop a new Enhanced CS Module. Simulator changes included addition of a retractable uterus and restricted pelvic inlet, and the fetal mannequin was modified to allow accurate limb articulation and flexion at the waist. Participants were individually recorded. Obstetricians were asked to deliver the fetal head, and midwives were asked to perform a vaginal push-up. Questionnaires assessed realism and usefulness for training using five-point Likert scale responses. Construct validity was assessed by testing the a priori hypothesis that experts would be more likely to achieve delivery than novices. Performance variables were compared between groups using Chi-square and Mann-Whitney U-tests. In all, 105 simulated scenarios were undertaken. A range of techniques were employed to deliver the impacted fetal head, including change of hand, vaginal disimpaction, and reverse breech extraction. The novel birth trainer allows rehearsal of all disimpaction techniques. Techniques for impacted fetal head are difficult to learn experientially, and simulation is likely to provide an effective and safe form of training.",
