  "other_facts": "Music can influence behavior across a range of diverse domains. Monitoring of internal timing mechanisms is influenced by tempo, liking, perceived affective nature, and everyday listening contexts. The seven sound conditions were: silence; car sounds; car sounds with self-selected music; car sounds with experimenter-selected high-arousal music at 70 bpm; car sounds with experimenter-selected high-arousal music at 130 bpm; car sounds with experimenter-selected low-arousal music at 70 bpm; and car sounds with experimenter-selected low-arousal music at 130 bpm. Six performance measures were taken: time, accuracy, speed, and retrospective perception of these. Four experience measures were taken: perceived distraction, liking, appropriateness, and enjoyment. Personal meaning and subjective associations connected to self-selected music were suggested to promote increased engagement with the activity, overriding detrimental effects attributed to unfamiliar, less liked, and less appropriate experimenter-selected music.",
  "summary": "In an experimental study of 70 participants playing a driving game, self-selected music improved accuracy and experience but distorted elapsed time perception, whereas experimenter-selected music worsened performance and experience, with faster high-arousal tempos increasing speed and inaccuracy."
