      "timepoint": "after emergent surgical thrombectomy",
      "effect": "restoration of arterial blood flow",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Cardiac metastatic squamous cell laryngeal carcinoma is rare. Blood pressure was 92/55 mmHg; heart rate was 112 bpm. Initial troponin was 0.27. Electrocardiogram showed sinus tachycardia with inverted T waves in the infero-lateral leads. Echocardiogram showed preserved left ventricular function with multiple areas of echogenic masses in all four cardiac chambers located at the annulus of the tricuspid valve, the right ventricular free wall, and along the interventricular septum. No intracardiac shunt was detected by contrast study. Computed tomography scan of the heart confirmed multiple exophytic intracardiac masses within the left atrium, right ventricle, interventricular septum, and lateral free wall of the left ventricle. Immunohistochemical staining with cytokeratin of the emboli was consistent with malignant squamous cell carcinoma consistent with metastases from known laryngeal squamous cell cancer.",
  "summary": "A 49-year-old man with recurrent squamous laryngeal carcinoma presented with acute right leg ischemia and multiple cardiac metastatic masses, and arterial blood flow was restored after emergent surgical thrombectomy."
}

