  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Ascorbate can act as both a reducing and oxidising agent in vitro depending on its environment. It can modulate the intracellular redox environment of cells and is predicted to modulate thiol-dependent cell signalling and gene expression pathways. The study compared protein changes elicited by ascorbate in vitro with in vivo effects on plasma potassium levels, PBMC apoptosis and PITP expression to investigate whether in vitro model systems are predictive of in vivo effects. No effects on plasma potassium levels were observed in supplemented subjects despite a reduction of K+ channel proteins in ascorbate-treated T cells in vitro. No effect of vitamin C supplementation on PBMC apoptosis was observed, whilst ascorbate decreased expression of caspase 3 recruitment domain protein in vitro. These data provide one of the first demonstrations that proteomics may be valuable in developing predictive markers of nutrient effects in vivo and may identify novel pathways for studying mechanisms of action in vivo.",
  "summary": "Vitamin C supplementation up to 2 g/day for 10 weeks increased PITP expression after 5 and 10 weeks but did not affect plasma potassium levels or PBMC apoptosis, showing limited concordance with ascorbate-induced protein changes observed in vitro."
}

