  "other_facts": "Non-eGFR indicators covered 2010-2024, eGFR covered 2012-2024. Data source: National Health Insurance Service (NHIS) health-screening aggregate tables distributed through KOSIS. Operational endpoints: eGFR < 60 mL/min/1.73 m2, serum creatinine ≥ 1.5 mg/dL, dipstick proteinuria ≥ 1+, triglycerides ≥ 150 mg/dL, and sex-specific anemia proxy. Triglycerides and anemia proxy treated as associated metabolic/hematological indicators. Crude national trends supplemented with direct age-standardization (to 2024 screening population) for eGFR, creatinine, proteinuria, and triglycerides. Sex-stratified trends for all indicators. Binomial logit GLM with calendar year as predictor generated 2025-2028 forecasts; forecast reliability assessed by temporal hold-out validation (fitting through 2020, predicting 2021-2024) and recent-window (2018-2024) sensitivity analysis; 95% bootstrap prediction intervals derived. Regional ecological correlations by Pearson and Spearman. Age-standardized prevalence of dipstick proteinuria ≥1+ in 2012: 2.34%. Age-standardized prevalence of eGFR<60 in 2012: 5.20%. Age-standardized decline for eGFR was steeper than crude decline, indicating finding not artifact of population aging. Hold-out validation mean absolute error 0.05-0.14 percentage points for eGFR, creatinine, and proteinuria; poor fit for triglycerides and anemia proxy (exploratory only). Jeonnam had highest crude regional eGFR<60 prevalence: 5.09% (regional comparisons not age-standardized). Regional eGFR<60 strongly correlated with elevated serum creatinine (Pearson r=0.954, p<0.001) and moderately correlated with anemia proxy (Pearson r=0.611, p=0.009). Conclusions support confirmatory testing after abnormal screening results and targeted kidney-risk follow-up, but should not be interpreted as confirmed CKD incidence or prevalence.",
  "summary": "This retrospective repeated cross-sectional ecological trend study using NHIS/KOSIS screening data from 2010-2024 found an upward trend in dipstick proteinuria and a decreasing trend in eGFR<60, with forecasts to 2028, supporting confirmatory testing after abnormal screening results."
}

