      "effect": "no differences between acrylamide-treated and control mice",
      "ci": null
    }
  ],
  "safety": null,
  "other_facts": "Acrylamide is a possible human carcinogen formed in certain carbohydrate-rich foods processed at high temperature. Diets were without acrylamide (control) or with acrylamide at doses reflecting upper levels found in human foods. Male F344 rats were randomized to diet groups. Two weeks after diet assignment, rats in each group received two weekly subcutaneous injections of azoxymethane or saline, and colons were assessed for tumors. Acrylamide by itself was not a complete carcinogen but acted as a co-carcinogen by exacerbating the effects of azoxymethane. In nude mice, acrylamide did not aid progression of established tumors. Food-borne acrylamide at levels comparable to those found in human foods was neither an independent carcinogen nor a tumor promoter in the colon, but may pose a potential hazard as a colon co-carcinogen in association with known and possibly other environmental tumor initiators/promoters.",
  "summary": "In male F344 rats, dietary acrylamide at 2 mg/kg diet increased AOM-induced colon tumor incidence, size, and burden without affecting multiplicity, while in nude mice it did not affect established human colon tumor xenograft growth, indicating co-carcinogenic but not independent carcinogenic or tumor-promoting effects."
}

