  "other_facts": "The erythropoietin resistance index (ERI) is described as an accurate indicator of erythropoietin (EPO) resistance and is related to worse prognosis in patients on hemodialysis; it is simple, cheap, and could be calculated easily in children receiving hemodialysis. The study aimed to assess EPO resistance in children with kidney failure on regular hemodialysis. ERI was calculated. Patients were assessed by history taking and laboratory investigations including complete blood count, C-reactive protein (CRP), iron, ferritin, parathyroid hormone, and serum electrolytes. Correlation P values: age P = 0.031; EPO dose P = 0.001; CRP P = 0.049; KT/V P = 0.019; hemoglobin level P = 0.001; platelet count P = 0.023. Erythropoietin resistance was associated with many risk factors, including high CRP and low KT/V; inadequate hemodialysis was reported as the most important risk factor for EPO resistance in children on chronic hemodialysis, and adequate hemodialysis was reported as a protective measure.",
  "summary": "In an analytical cross-sectional study of 80 children with kidney failure on regular hemodialysis, mean ERI was 28.87 ± 10.62 and ERI correlated positively with age, EPO dose, and CRP, and negatively with KT/V, hemoglobin, and platelet count."
}

