  "other_facts": "IPC is an iron preparation with non-ionic iron and polymaltose in a stable complex; its usefulness in treating iron deficiency anemia has recently been a topic of much debate. The overview reviewed published comparative literature versus ferrous sulfate as reference using standard Cochrane group methods and criteria. The aim was to establish differences in efficacy (primary end-point: hemoglobin after approx. 2 months of treatment) and safety (number of patients with adverse drug reactions). From 14 comparative trials identified, 6 adult comparative studies (1 double blind) were retained; 4 pediatric studies initially selected were rejected because of heterogeneity of data at baseline. IPC was compared with ferrous sulfate in equivalent doses. The meta-analysis showed similar hemoglobin levels and similar efficacy in adults. Tolerance of IPC in adults was clearly better than ferrous sulfate, with significant differences for individual adverse reactions, suggesting a better risk/benefit ratio of IPC in adults. Properly conducted randomized controlled trials, particularly in pediatrics, are needed.",
